docs/decisions.md:1254:**Only the project owner decides these items, OD1 – OD157.** All are answered. OD1 – OD32 (exit 2n, closed) and OD47 were decided on 2026-10-29 (OD23 – OD32 by the strictness pass of that day). OD33 – OD157 were decided on 2026-10-31, by the owner's rule that an answer every reviewer agreed on and as strict as Austral or stricter is taken, and on 2026-11-01, when the owner approved the final set for the rest; the authority decision of the same day supersedes OD38's and OD156's `Root` readings (OD94 was withdrawn: MOD-1 already refuses its case, bug 337). The answers are recorded in [docs/decisions.md](decisions.md), state the decided rules in chapters now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), then become Lean in phases B–D, and are enforced as each new-compiler slice lands. The questions raised while recording them were answered on 2026-11-02, the owner deciding the first four and, by the owner's rule of that day, Claude and Codex agreeing on the best long-term answer for the rest ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02)); they revise OD24, OD38, OD43, OD83, OD102, OD146 and OD156 and the authority rules. The spec audit's questions were answered the same day ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)), revising OD140 and STMT-13. Traits are exported and imported like any other declaration (owner, 2026-11-02). Core was provisionally approved on 2026-11-05 ([record](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); A2b formalization is current, and the A2c freeze awaits the four design theorems, the A2i integration stage (2026-11-06) and an outside-reader pass. New questions follow the same rule: No agent, orchestrator or
docs/decisions.md:1293:| OD3 | <a id="od3"></a>Pattern binders through a borrowed scrutinee: the binder is always `&T`; its mode is stored in Core; the root cannot be reassigned while a binder's loan lives (the rule that reopened G17's Copy case) | D3 and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (REF-9), and with it the refusal of G17's Copy case (closed 2026-10-23 in the references chapter of the specification) | exits 2i (G17) and 2k (explicit match binders), so every deliverable; bug 181's repair (exit 0b); R-0496 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the binder is always `&T`; its mode is stored in Core; the root cannot be reassigned while a binder's loan lives. Already enforced (REF-9, PASS-2, REF-8) |
docs/decisions.md:1298:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1304:| OD14 | <a id="od14"></a>Type validity (G2, G13, G15, G16): one structural well-formedness judgment before specialization; function types that return a reference refused everywhere | T-G2, T-G13, T-G15, T-G16; implemented 2026-10-23 (G13, G15 and G16 recorded closed in the references chapter of the specification) | exit 2i, so every deliverable | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: one structural well-formedness judgment runs before specialization, and function types that return a reference are refused everywhere |
docs/decisions.md:1328:| OD38 | <a id="od38"></a>The root resource API (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD8). Options: `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` with trusted splitting functions (`root.split_alloc()`, `root.split_console()`, ...) returning linear tokens; or a fixed set of token parameters `main` may declare (`fn main(alloc: Alloc, io: Console)`). Recommendation: the single `Root` value, as Austral's `RootCapability`, split by trusted functions the wrapper gate lists Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` (or Unit), `Root` split into linear tokens by trusted functions; specify the split functions and how leftover authority is consumed | `main` takes no parameters today (STMT-7) | R-0557 (TRUST-9) and STMT-7 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: there is no `Root` type; `main`'s parameters are the target's singletons, hosted `fn main() with(...) -> u8` and freestanding `fn main(uart: Uart, arena: Arena) with(Device, Alloc) -> u8`; revised 2026-11-02: `main` receives the target's grants and returns `u8` only ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1329:| OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate |
docs/decisions.md:1332:| OD42 | <a id="od42"></a>Comparisons on `bool` and `char` under OD26 (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26, whose answer says "arithmetic, shifts and comparisons take numbers only"). Two readings: numbers only, so `b == true` and `c < 'z'` are refused (a `bool` is tested directly, a `char` converted by a named function); or "numbers" excludes pointers only, and `bool` and `char` keep equality (and `char` ordering). Recommendation: keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, and refuse only pointers, which is what OD26's alignment with TRUST-1 is about Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, refuse ordering on `bool`; amend TYPE-6 and OD26 explicitly | the current compiler compares `bool` and `char` | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `==`/`!=` on bool/char and `<` on char; refuse ordering on bool |
docs/decisions.md:1354:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
docs/decisions.md:1357:| OD67 | <a id="od67"></a>Locals that reuse a top-level name (added 2026-10-30, item review new 4 (SCOPE-4) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a local binding may reuse the name of a top-level function, an import alias or an intrinsic (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.4 also lists constants). Options: (a) allowed, as today; (b) refused, as SCOPE-1 refuses shadowing a local. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | allowed | SCOPE-1, SCOPE-2, MOD-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
docs/decisions.md:1387:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1392:| OD102 | <a id="od102"></a>`Float32` and `Char` as type spellings (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether `Float32` and `Char` are other spellings of `f32` and `char`, as `Float64` and `Bool` are of `f64` and `bool`. Bears on TYPE-19, SYN-3. The current compiler: the parser maps `Float64` and `Bool` to `f64` and `bool` but not `Float32` or `Char`, so `fn g(x: f32) -> Float32 { return x; }` is refused (E0220) while the resolver's `tyFromName` maps all four. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §1 names `Float32`/`f32` as two spellings. Options: (a) `Float32` and `Char` become spellings of `f32` and `char`; (b) neither, keeping the current compiler's `Float64` and `Bool`; (c) `Float32` and `Char` decided separately. Whether every capitalized spelling (`Int`, `Uint`, `Float64`, `Bool`) is removed is a separate removal question, not asked here. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-102 | see OPEN-102 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `Float32` or `Char` aliases. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: the aliases `Int`, `Uint`, `Float64` and `Bool` are removed too ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-aliases)) |
docs/decisions.md:1393:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
docs/decisions.md:1404:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
docs/decisions.md:1408:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
docs/decisions.md:1415:| OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1416:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
docs/decisions.md:1417:| OD127 | <a id="od127"></a>UTF-8 in every `String` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether every `String` value that safe code can reach holds valid UTF-8, as a language guarantee. Bears on TYPE-3, SYN-5, TRUST-2. The current compiler: literals are UTF-8 (SYN-5), but nothing keeps a `String` valid: `push_char` appends a byte unchecked, `Bytes::to_string` transfers bytes without validation, and std's `read_to_string` does not validate. [STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md) §1 states the invariant as a target, and [UNICODE_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/UNICODE_POLICY.md) has a validating `Bytes::to_string` returning `Option`. Options: (a) a language guarantee, every safe constructor validating and an unchecked one being a trusted operation with that premise; (b) a std convention only, `String` being bytes to the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-127 | see OPEN-127 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Language guarantee |
docs/decisions.md:1427:| OD137 | <a id="od137"></a>Character literals outside ASCII (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which byte a char literal denotes when its character is not ASCII. Bears on SYN-6, TYPE-20 and SYN-5. The current compiler: the literal silently takes the low byte of the character's code point: `'é'` (U+00E9) is 233, `'ā'` (U+0101) is 1 and `'€'` (U+20AC) is 172 as `u8`, on both consumers, and SYN-6 says only that a char literal is a token. Options: (a) refuse a char literal whose character is not ASCII, so every accepted literal is the one byte of its ASCII character; (b) accept U+0000 to U+00FF as that byte (Latin-1) and refuse anything above; (c) make `char` a Unicode scalar value, which changes TYPE-20's one-byte `char`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-137 | see OPEN-137 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII |
docs/decisions.md:1439:| OD149 | <a id="od149"></a>How `[e; N]` evaluates `e` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How many times an array repetition `[e; N]` evaluates `e`, and which `e` it accepts. Bears on STMT-4, OWN-15 and TYPE-7. The current compiler: `e` is evaluated once per element, in index order (`[bump(&mut n); 3]` gives `[1, 2, 3]` on both consumers), and whether a linear `e` is accepted was not shown (an array of a linear type that is never consumed is refused with E0208 either way). OWN-15 says only that an array literal moves each element in, and STMT-4 orders written elements. Options: (a) `e` is evaluated once and copied, so it must be Copy; (b) `e` is evaluated `N` times in index order, as the current compiler does; (c) `e` must be a constant expression. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-149 | see OPEN-149 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Evaluated once, must be Copy |
docs/decisions.md:1444:| OD154 | <a id="od154"></a>Whether the string builtins require `Alloc` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `string_concat`, `string_slice`, `string_substr`, `string_trim`, `int_to_string`, `bool_to_string`, `float_to_string`, `string_push_char`, `string_append`, `string_append_int` and `string_append_bool` require the `Alloc` capability. Bears on MEM-7, TRUST-4 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: Check gives none of these builtins a capability, and a function with no capabilities calls `string_concat` and `string_append_int` and exits 42 on both consumers (the other names were not probed). MEM-7@1 lists `string_reserve` and `append` among the builtins that require `Alloc`; `append` is accepted without `Alloc` (bug 349), and it expands into `string_append`, `string_append_int`, `string_append_bool` and `string_push_char`. MEM-7's rationale says allocation is an effect a signature shows. Options: (a) each builtin named above requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists it; (b) a stated subset of them requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists that subset; (c) only the builtins MEM-7 already lists require `Alloc`, so `string_reserve` and `append` do and the names above do not, and MEM-7 says so; (d) no string builtin requires `Alloc`, `string_reserve` and `append` included, and MEM-7 drops those two. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-154 | see OPEN-154 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allocating string builtins need `Alloc` (MEM-7; closes bug 349) |
docs/decisions.md:1446:| OD156 | <a id="od156"></a>The capability of `get_args` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Which capability the builtin `get_args` requires, or whether the builtin is removed. Bears on TRUST-4, STMT-7, TRUST-9 and OPEN-136. The current compiler: Check gives `get_args()` the result `HeapArray<String>` and requires `Process` (E0520 with `Env` only); the builtin has no implementation on either consumer (bug 347). STMT-7's rationale says command-line arguments come from `std.args` with `Env`, and OD8 gives `main` the root resource value (TRUST-9). Options: (a) `Env`, as `std.args` requires; (b) `Process`, as the current compiler's signature says; (c) another capability, or none; (d) remove the builtin, and command-line arguments come from `std.args`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-156 | see OPEN-156 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: `get_args` requires `with(Env)`, and `with(Alloc)` when it allocates. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: it also takes the `Environment` grant, and an allocator when it allocates ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem)) |
docs/decisions.md:1461:  and this is not duplication. `Std` is removed as a capability: every signature, `main` included,
docs/decisions.md:1464:  and OD83's allocator token. OD8 stands except where this supersedes it: its root value and
docs/decisions.md:1484:  value (a function or constant) in one module may not share a name. A struct literal `S { ... }` and
